        "original_id": "fca90e11-c126-56e9-834f-078b6fce7905",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Robert A. Millikan, Harvey Fletcher\n- Experiments: oil drop experiment\n- Concepts: elementary electric charge, electron charge, electrostatic levitation, photoelectric effect\n- Projects: Gravity Probe B\n- Awards: Nobel Prize in Physics\n\nFacts:\n- In 1909, Robert A. Millikan and Harvey Fletcher performed the oil drop experiment to measure the elementary electric charge, which is the charge of the electron.\n- Robert Andrews Millikan was born on March 22, 1868, and died on December 19, 1953.\n- In 1923, Robert Andrews Millikan received the Nobel Prize in Physics for measuring the elementary electronic charge and for his work on the photoelectric effect.\n- Electrostatic levitation was used in Robert Millikan's oil drop experiment.\n- Electrostatic levitation is used to suspend the gyroscopes in Gravity Probe B during launch.",
